brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
@buildthis.bisks.net add a way to click and drag strands around.

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@shimmermathlabs.com: early fluoddity user from 1884 investigating braid engineering with pen and paper

en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knot_th...
  [quoting @all-paperclips.bsky.social: I'm trying to build some machinery for engineering the braids. This shows a simple hand drawn guide track which has captured a single thread. Braiding/weaving will probably involve alternating striped guides, or I might try to make a bunch of stable loops and shove them together.]
  [image, alt text: tenfold knottiness
a grid of knots, 13x14, each knot is a loop path that intersects itself many times in different patterns.]
@gracekind.net: @norvid-studies.bsky.social
@shimmermathlabs.com: @buildthis.bisks.net a knot visualizer. user picks size N, site generates random permutations of [1..N]. these permutations are used to draw knots using petal projection (grid of them). then use some sort of energy relaxation to let the strings animate and look more like knots.
@buildthis.bisks.net: a grid of knot diagrams grown from petal projections — pick N, each card is a fresh random permutation of 1..N, relaxing under a live spring simulation so the strands settle and wobble like real string 🌸

built it 🎉 — https://petalgarden.bisks.net

